broken-mirror-70223100.limits_and_boundary:
As the universe approaches absolute zero temperature (T → 0 K), if a departure from thermal equilibrium exists, any generated baryon asymmetry (
      		    n_B - n_{\bar{B}}
      		  ) would be preserved, provided the Sakharov conditions remain active. Conversely, if the universe were in perfect thermal equilibrium (T → ∞ in terms of equilibrium rate, though temperature is a measure of energy), then 
      		    n_B ≈ n_{\bar{B}}
      		  due to the symmetry of forward and inverse reactions, implying 
      		    n_B - n_{\bar{B}} = 0
      		  unless other mechanisms are at play. The absence of any of the Sakharov conditions would also lead to 
      		    n_B - n_{\bar{B}} = 0
      		 .

broken-mirror-70223100.conceptual_definition:
The equation "Broken Mirror" encapsulates the fundamental requirement for baryogenesis, the process that created the observed dominance of matter over antimatter in the universe. It states that a universe will evolve to have a net asymmetry between matter particles (
      		    n_B
      		  ) and antimatter particles (
      		    n_{\bar{B}}
      		  ) if and only if the three Sakharov conditions are met. These conditions are necessary and sufficient for generating and maintaining a matter-antimatter asymmetry from an initially symmetric state.
